<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- eldato conflict-rebase — the SAME pass the Linux cells run on a systemd timer (#2698).
         Scheduled here by scripts/install-conflict-rebase-timer.sh, which renders this file and
         bootstraps it. ONE install verb, the platform's own scheduler (#2058).

         WHY THIS FILE IS NOT IN templates/launchd/. scripts/install-launchd.sh installs EVERY
         templates/launchd/*.plist with NO arguments and a DIFFERENT substitution set, and
         pi-bootstrap/setup.sh runs it on every Darwin box; scripts/fleet-schedule.py reads the same
         top-level glob on a cell. A copy there would render with its placeholders unresolved, be
         SKIPPED with a warning, and the box would silently have no scheduled pass — or, on a cell
         that also runs the systemd timer, TWO passes on one queue. The subdirectory keeps this file
         out of both globs (templates/launchd/reaper/ and harness-apply/ set the same precedent).

         NO PLACEHOLDER MAY APPEAR INSIDE AN XML COMMENT. A doubled dash is forbidden in XML text, so
         an option flag written with its dashes here would make the installed plist unparseable,
         launchd would refuse it, and the box would silently have no pass. The installer refuses a
         rendered plist that does not parse, which is the read-back that makes this safe.

         WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS THE CHECKOUT'S OWN PYTHON, NOT bash. macOS TCC is per-binary: a
         launchd-spawned bash cannot read a script under ~/Documents, and a launchd-spawned git
         cannot read a repo there. This pass is git worktree add plus git push against that checkout,
         so an entry point of bash would fail closed every interval while launchctl list showed a
         healthy job. The interpreter that holds the access reads the script and hands it to bash on
         stdin (the same launcher install-disk-reaper-timer.sh and install-harness-apply-timer.sh
         use). No usable interpreter is a REFUSAL at install time.

         THIRTY MINUTES, matching the systemd timer. The pass is bounded by the repo's own
         lib/bounded.sh and caps itself at CONFLICT_REBASE_MAX PRs per run.

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: the pass writes its own log and beacon under the state
         dir, and an unbounded launchd-captured log is the symlink-truncation surface the reapers
         already guard. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>{{LABEL}}</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{PY_BIN}}</string>
        <string>{{LAUNCHER}}</string>
        <string>--script</string>
        <string>{{TIMER}}</string>
        <string>start</string>
        <string>--once</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <!-- The checkout is PINNED, never resolved from the job's environment: a launchd job
             inherits none of a login shell's rc files, so leaving it ambient would act on a different
             tree and say so nowhere. -->
        <key>CONFLICT_REBASE_CHECKOUT</key>
        <string>{{CHECKOUT}}</string>
        <key>CONFLICT_REBASE_REPO</key>
        <string>{{REPO}}</string>
        <key>CONFLICT_REBASE_MAX</key>
        <string>{{MAX_PRS}}</string>
        <key>CONFLICT_REBASE_INCLUDE_BEHIND</key>
        <string>{{INCLUDE_BEHIND}}</string>
        <key>CONFLICT_REBASE_STATE</key>
        <string>{{STATE}}</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>{{INTERVAL_SEC}}</integer>
    <!-- A pass AT LOAD as well as on the interval, so a freshly installed agent has a beacon within
         seconds instead of after the first interval. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- A background pass: it must not compete with a lane for CPU or I/O. -->
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
